adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1+r7ios1+r7vol1+r7log1+r7ssh2+r7phosh1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Fix the fresh-flash black-screen-forever hang. Root cause, traced live
    2026-08-08 via a persistent-journal capture of the exact hung boot:
    phoc's libseat session-activation wait (hardcoded ~10s, not
    configurable) timed out under a heavily-loaded first boot (audio/WiFi/
    modem/sensor errors firing concurrently), so phoc failed to create a
    backend and exited. That alone is recoverable (phosh.service has
    Restart=always/RestartSec=5s), but stock phosh.service also has
    OnFailure=getty@tty1.service + Conflicts=getty@tty1.service - once the
    OnFailure fallback starts a getty, Conflicts= permanently blocks
    phosh.service's own restart attempts for the rest of that boot (zero
    restarts observed in the 107s window following the failure, though
    RestartSec=5s should have produced ~20). Screen stays black and fully
    unresponsive - no compositor is running to receive input - until a
    full reboot gives phosh an unconflicted attempt. Drop the Conflicts=
    via a service.d override so Restart=always can actually self-heal;
    keep OnFailure= as a debug console for genuinely persistent failures.

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 20:30:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1+r7ios1+r7vol1+r7log1+r7ssh2) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Fix sshd taking minutes to start listening on a fresh flash, which
    looked like "SSH doesn't work over USB" but affected every interface
    equally (WiFi tests just happened to run later, after sshd had
    finally started). Root cause: r7-regenerate-ssh-host-keys.service
    (Before=ssh.service/ssh.socket, must finish before sshd binds so it
    never serves the image's baked-in shared keys) ran `ssh-keygen -A`,
    which also generates an RSA host key - entropy-hungry, and this image
    has no haveged/rng-tools, so on a genuinely fresh flash (zero
    accumulated kernel entropy) it stalled sshd's actual startup by
    minutes. Traced live 2026-08-08 via TWRP (mounting the LV read-only
    over the same USB cable, no network involved) after ruling out
    sshd_config, /etc/default/ssh, ssh.socket and nftables.conf - all
    completely unrestricted. Generate only ed25519+ecdsa host keys
    instead (negligible entropy need, near-instant even at zero
    entropy); sshd_config has no directive requiring an RSA host key.

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1+r7ios1+r7vol1+r7log1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Make the systemd journal persistent (Storage=persistent), overriding the
    base image's 10-journald-volatile.conf which kept every boot's journal
    in tmpfs only. Root cause of an undiagnosable failure: a fresh-flash
    first-boot hit a totally black, power-button-unresponsive screen; by the
    time we could reach the device (force-restart), that boot's entire
    journal was already gone (tmpfs, wiped on reboot) - only a separate,
    unrelated kernel Oops from the *next* boot survived, in pstore. Without
    persistent journal storage this class of first-boot failure is
    permanently undiagnosable after the fact. postinst flushes the current
    runtime journal to disk immediately so this takes effect without an
    extra reboot.

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1+r7ios1+r7vol1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Fix audio volume resetting to full on every restart/reboot. Root cause
    (confirmed against the actual PulseAudio v14.2 source): module-stream-
    restore's only subscription is
    PA_SUBSCRIPTION_MASK_SINK_INPUT|PA_SUBSCRIPTION_MASK_SOURCE_OUTPUT - it
    has no code path at all for a sink's own device volume, only
    per-application stream volumes. There is nothing droid-sink or this
    port's config is doing wrong; stock PulseAudio simply has no mechanism
    to persist sink volume (PipeWire+WirePlumber, which this device does
    not use, handles that differently).

    Add two small user services: r7-volume-save watches `pactl subscribe`
    for sink volume-change events and writes the percentage to
    ~/.local/state/r7-volume; r7-volume-restore applies the saved value (or
    a 70% default on first boot) at PulseAudio startup. Proven live across
    two full change/restart/verify cycles (33% and 77%, both held exactly
    across a `systemctl --user restart pulseaudio.service`).

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 17:00:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1+r7ios1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Fix consuming an iPhone's Personal Hotspot over USB. Root cause: the
    iPhone enumerates in a default PTP-only USB configuration (one
    interface, matches the "allow access to photos" prompt) until usbmuxd
    negotiates the lockdown/pairing handshake and the device switches to
    its full "PTP + Apple Mobile Device + Apple USB Ethernet"
    configuration - only then does ipheth (already built into this kernel)
    have an interface to bind. usbmuxd wasn't installed (only its client
    libraries were, pulled in transitively); its own udev rule
    (39-usbmuxd.rules) auto-activates it on Apple vendor ID match, so
    installing the package is the entire fix, no adaptation-side
    wiring needed. Proven live end-to-end 2026-08-08: after installing
    usbmuxd and accepting the iPhone's Trust prompt, ipheth bound, DHCP
    lease from the phone's 172.20.10.0/28 hotspot subnet, ping and general
    internet access confirmed over the USB link (GUNLUK G1.32).
  * Bump the default outgoing IP TTL 64 -> 65 (net.ipv4.ip_default_ttl,
    system-wide). When this device consumes another phone's
    tethering/Personal Hotspot (iPhone, over USB ipheth above or Bluetooth
    PAN), the tethering phone forwards our packets and decrements TTL by
    one hop; carriers can fingerprint that as tethered/shared usage and
    bill or throttle differently. Shipping TTL 65 means it reads as the
    phone's own native 64 by the time it reaches the carrier. Global
    rather than interface-scoped: ipheth's interface name is MAC-derived
    per iPhone (no stable name to match), Bluetooth PAN uses bnep0, and
    TTL 65 has no functional downside on any other path (Wi-Fi, mobile
    data) - a system-wide default is the non-fragile choice over
    interface-matching iptables/nftables rules.

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 16:30:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2+r7usb1) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Fix USB RNDIS networking, which was completely non-functional (only
    charging worked over USB). Root cause: mtp-server's mtp-configfs script
    runs automatically at every boot (mtp-configfs@rndis.service, after
    android_boot_completed.service) but immediately exits with "MTP not
    supported" - for ANY requested mode, not just mtp - unless
    /usr/lib/droidian/device/mtp-supported exists; nothing shipped it, so
    the USB gadget was never configured at all despite the kernel having
    full configfs/DWC3/RNDIS support (CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_RNDIS=y etc.) and
    the controller enumerating fine. Ship that marker plus
    /etc/hybris-usb/dhcpd.conf (10.15.19.0/24, matching the static IP the
    script assigns the gadget interface) and pull in isc-dhcp-server for
    its dhcpd binary, which the script invokes directly; the package's own
    generic isc-dhcp-server.service is unused (no interface configured
    there) and gets disabled in postinst so it doesn't fail at every boot.
    Proven live end-to-end 2026-08-08: rndis0 comes up automatically at
    boot, host gets a DHCP lease, ping and SSH work over the USB link
    (GUNLUK G1.30/G1.31).

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 15:15:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup3+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * r7-setup-lock: fire directly at graphical-session start instead of
    behind r7-setup-lock.path (PathExists=~/.config/gnome-initial-setup-done).
    Live-diagnosed 2026-08-08 (GUNLUK G1.30): gnome-initial-setup's
    --existing-user first-login mode writes that marker within ~2s of
    session start, not at real wizard completion (the file gets touched
    again ~3.5 minutes later at true completion) — so the .path fired
    almost immediately anyway, then kept re-arming every time the
    (permanently true) marker was re-touched, hit systemd's trigger-limit,
    and disabled itself (journal: "Trigger limit hit, refusing further
    activation"). The DNS probe this gating was originally protecting
    (waiting for GIS's Wi-Fi step) was already dropped in +r7setup2
    (GUNLUK G1.18), so nothing here needs GIS to run first anymore. Removed
    r7-setup-lock.path; postinst now enables r7-setup-lock.service
    directly (already had WantedBy=graphical-session.target) and disables
    any leftover .path enablement from an older install.
  * r7-setup-lock: reword for the corrected order (lock now intentionally
    shown before language/Wi-Fi/account setup, not after) — "Setting up
    your device" / "Your phone is ready" / "Start using your phone" implied
    total completion when a user setup step was still ahead. Now "Preparing
    your device" / "Device patching done" / "Begin setup".

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0300

adaptation-samsung-r7 (0.0.0+git20260720203123.c4c06d0.bookworm+git20260728092037.c349ebc.bookworm+r7spen1+r7dns1+r7setup2+r7ssh1+r7spenapp2+r7desktop2) bookworm; urgency=medium

  * Desktop-shell: add r7-auto-rotate.service (follows the accelerometer via
    net.hadess.SensorProxy, applies wlr-randr --transform) and REMOVE the
    libinput touch calibration matrix entirely. A prior version of this
    package had concluded wlroots doesn't apply the output transform to touch
    input and compensated with a per-rotation libinput calibration matrix
    (written to a volatile /run udev rule by r7-switch-session and
    r7-auto-rotate). That conclusion was wrong: wlroots DOES apply the output
    transform to touch internally, so the matrix was double-rotating touch,
    landing taps mirrored/inverted (right edge activated the leftmost waybar
    icon). Proven live (2026-08-04, including a full device reboot to rule out
    session-state corruption as an alternative explanation): with NO
    calibration matrix at all, touch is correctly aligned in every rotation.
    r7-switch-session and r7-auto-rotate are both simplified accordingly (no
    more calibration file writes or targeted udev retriggers).

 -- Yunus Tan <yunus.tan@outlook.com.tr>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 16:30:49 +0300

